 us to  solve alone  we're just ideologically speaking we  think it is the right thing to do so  it's good for the world and it is good  for us so switching from that sort of  rosy perspective let's take a look at a  dark doomsday scenario done done a day  without open stories so we use open  source a lot at T Sigma we use pandas  and Jupiter and numpy to do our analysis  to name just a few we use spark for  distributed computing we use git to  store our code and if these projects  were no longer being actively maintained  and developed there's a lot of stuff we  would no longer be able to do and we're  not the only ones in this position I  would redo that most companies in the  fortune 500 depend on some sort of open  source even if it's just Linux servers  which by the way are also powering our  PlayStation 3 Playstation 4 and intend  to switch all sorts of things so if this  infrastructure just disappeared  a lot of things would just grind to a  halt as one recent example Theano a  numerical computation library on Python  just announced that within a year work  is going to stop entirely on the project  so this was interesting news for other  projects like PI MC 3 and caris who are  designed around Theano and now have to  rethink what they're gonna do so I  couldn't say for sure whether if the a  knows shuttering has anything to do with  the lack of financial support but what I  do know is that they're using  development does have a widespread  effect and Theano isn't an isolated  example so there's been a that probably  the most important sentence in this talk  which is that the public digital  infrastructure is not free and it's not  invulnerable a lot of the projects that  are most widely used don't enjoy  widespread support looking at number for  example they receive their first grant  from the Moore Foundation just this past  summer  so these projects that we all depend on  they don't just appear for free they  need developer time and that needs money  and th